## ABSTRACT
Hollow metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted wide interest because of their unique core-shell structure. As a novel uranium adsorbent, diaminomaleonitrile (DAMN) functionalized double-shelled hollow (DSHM) metal-organic framework of chromium(III) terephthalate was prepared by a post-synthetic method of grafting amino group onto coordinatively unsaturated sites (CUS). TEM, XRD, nitrogen sorption and FT-IR spectrometry were used to characterize the obtained DAMN functionalized hollow MOF. The maximum capacity of uranium adsorption is $601~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , which exceeds all known functionalized MIL-101 uranium adsorption materials. The optimal adsorption pH is 8 which is close to the pH of seawater. Furthermore, the high selectivity of uranium and high adsorption rate in simulated seawater provide a promising prospect to introduce DSHM-DAMN for the extraction of uranium from seawater. In this work, we first applied hollow MOF for uranium adsorption, which greatly improves the uranium adsorption performance of MOF materials; we also explored the practical application of hollow MOF materials.
KEYWORDS: hollow metal-organic framework, uranium extraction, coordinatively unsaturated sites, simulated seawater
## INTRODUCTION
Nowadays, nuclear energy has attracted much attention because of the increased demand for clean and higher density energy. The key for the long-term development of nuclear energy is utitilize the resource of Uranium, the main source of which at present are terrestrial ores that could run out in less than a hundred years at the current rate of consumption.1 However, the uranium in oceans is about 4.5 billion tons, which is 1000 times the reserves of uranium on land. But the low concentration (about 3.3ppb) and amounts of coexisting metal ions severely restrain the exploitation of this resource.2 Many kinds of adsorbent materials have been applied in the extraction of uranium from the ocean,3 however, a significant challenge remains to adopt a material which effectively selects and recovers uranium from the ocean.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of hybrid crystalline materials, which utilize metal ions and organic ligands to construct a 3D network structure via coordinated bonds.4 Owing to their ultrahigh surface area, tunable structure and controlled porosity, high crystallinity and stability,5 they has been widely investigated and used in many areas.6-9 Also, several types of MOFs have shown promise for efficient recovery of toxic and radioactive ions including uranium; most of these MOFs have been used as adsorbents after functionalization by organic groups.6, 10-15 MIL-101(Cr) is a well-known MOF with two types of mesoporous zeotypic cages and microporous windows, having high stability in aqueous solutions.16 Furthermore, as a substrate material, MIL-101 possesses unsaturated Cr (III) sites,17 which chelate with electron-rich organic groups. From previous reports, several N-containing groups have been introduced on to MIL-101 by means of coordinatively unsaturated sites (CUS).10, 18-20 In particular, N atoms have good selectivity toward uranium.21-22 Nevertheless, even taking into account these beneficial properties, the adsorption amount of functionalized MIL-101 series materials has never exceeded 500mg L-1. Arising from the above, we endeavor to enhance the adsorption capacity of functionalization MIL-101 which use the CUS, enabling availability of numerous accessible metal sites and contact of uranium with functional groups.
Hollow nanostructure materials, with their unique core-shell structure, have been widely applied in many areas, such as drug delivery, catalysis, adsorption of toxic ions.23-27 Recently, Huo et al. have reported on the construction of hollow MIL-10128 and found that the increased catalytic activity is most likely due to the exposure of the metal sites in each layer especially in the case of the decrease in hollow MIL-101’s surface area. Also, the hierarchical structure provides more accessible sites than those reported in other articles.29-30 Therefore, amine groups can easily graft onto the multi-shelled MIL-101 with numerous accessible unsaturated metal sites; its hierarchical structure enhances the contact of uranium with nitrogen atoms.
In our work, we prepared a double-shelled hollow MIL-101(DSHM) by means of a two-step crystal growth and subsequent acetic acid etching progress. To improve the uranium selectivity and adsorption capacity of DSHM, we used a post-synthetic method mentioned above, modifying it with the addition of diaminomaleonitrile (DAMN) which contains two nucleophilic $\left.-\mathrm{NH}_{_{2}}\right.$ and -CN groups that both coordinate with metal sites and uranium. The obtained product was characterized by XRD, nitrogen sorption, FT-IR spectrometry and transmission electron microscope (TEM). We studied in detail the adsorption of uranium on varying pH, contact times and different concentrations of uranium solution. We discuss the mechanism of uranium adsorption. The adsorption performance of our adsorbent was stable under five cycles of adsorption-desorption. Finally, we studied the selectivity of uranium adsorption under the condition of competing ions for the separation of uranium from simulated seawater.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Chemical Materials: $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}.9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and terephthalic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC)}$ were purchased from Aladdin Ltd. Diaminomaleonitrile and acetic acid were purchased from Alfa Aesar. Dimethylforamide (DMF) and rthanol were purchased from Alfa Aesar. All of the chemical materials were used without further purification, and we used deionized water for all the solutions during the experiments.
Double-shelled MIL-101 was synthesized in a step-by-step growth and subsequent etching progress. Briefly, $\ensuremath{0.8\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathrm{Cr(NO_{3})_{3}}{\bullet}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $_{0.22\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ were dissolved in $10\mathrm{ml}$ deionized water. The resulting mixture was carefully stirred for 1 h under room temperature and then transferred into a Teflon-lined autoclave for reaction at 453K for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . After reaction, the products were collected by centrifugation and then washed with DMF and ethanol. The products were added to $20~\mathrm{ml}$ deionized water (solution 1), then $10~\mathrm{ml}$ samples were taken from solution 1, and another $0.8\mathrm{~g~}$ of $\mathrm{Cr(NO_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9H_{2}O}$ and $\ensuremath{0.22\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ added to them. The mixture was carefully stirred for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ under room temperature, then poured into a Teflon-lined autoclave for reaction at 453K for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . The products were treated in the same way as above. Finally, the products were added to $20~\mathrm{ml}$ deionized water (solution 2). 2 ml acetic acid added to solution 2 under stirring, then the resulting suspension was transferred into a Teflon-lined autoclave and reacted at 453K for $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ . Finally, the products were collected through centrifugation and followed by washing with DMF and deionized water, and then dried at $393\mathrm{k}$ for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ under vacuum.
The synthesis of DSHM-DAMN used a direct post-grafting method to coordinate N atoms with unsaturated metal sites, as shown in Fig 3. In a typical procedure, $0.5\mathrm{~g~}$ DSHM and 0.15 mmol DAMN were suspended in anhydrous toluene ( $30~\mathrm{mL})$ in a flask, and the mixture refluxed at 393K with stirring for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . The products were collected by centrifugation and followed by washing with ethanol five times. Finally, the products were dried at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$
Characterization of the synthesized DSHM-DAMN: The synthesized material was measured by FTIR spectroscopic analysis using standard KBr pellets to confirm the grafting of organic groups. Then XRD analysis was performed on a Rigaku D/max-IIIB diffractometer (XRD, JEOL D/max-IIIB diffractometer, Japan) with CuKαirradiation $(\lambda{=}1.54178\mathrm{\A})$ , operated at $40\mathrm{kV}$ and $150~\mathrm{mA}$ at a range of $0.5\substack{-5^{\circ}}$ and $5.80^{\circ}(2\Theta)$ to determine the phosphorous content of our material. In addition, we conducted nitrogen sorption experiments under $77\mathrm{~k~}$ using a Micromeritics ASAP 2010 instrument, and the specific surface areas were calculated by using Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) method. TEM (EM, FEI Tecnai G2 S-Twin) was used to map the morphology of pristine MIL-101 and double-shelled MIL-101. XPS characterization was performed using ESCALAB 250Xi. ICP-AES and ICP-MS were used to determine the concentration of uranium both in ppm or ppb level.
A series of batch adsorption experiments were carried out to investigate the adsorption performance of DSHM-DAMN. All of the uranium solutions were diluted from a pristine uranium solution $({1000}\mathrm{mg}{\mathrm{L}}^{{-1}})$ , which was made by dissolving $2.11\ \mathrm{g}$ $\mathrm{UO}_{2}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{.}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in $1000~\mathrm{{mL}}$ deionized water. The influence of $\mathsf{p H}$ of the uranium solution, contact time, initial uranium concentration, adsorption temperature and coexisting ions were all investigated. The pH of the solution was adjusted by adding an appropriate amount of 0.1 M ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ and NaOH solution. The mixture was introduced into a conical flask in a $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ thermostatic shaker bath, and the supernatant was collected through filter. The concentration of supernatant was analyzed by ICP-OES. The adsorption capacity ${\sf q}_{\mathrm{e}}$ $\mathrm{~,~}(\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}})$ and the removal rate $(\%)$ of uranium were calculated according to following eqs. (1) and (2).
$$
q_{e}=(C_{0}-C_{e})\cdot(v/m)
$$
$$
\mathrm{Adsorption}\mathrm{rate}(\%)=(C_{0}-C_{e})/C_{0}\times100\%
$$
Where ${\sf q}_{\mathrm{e}}$ is the adsorption capacity of the adsorbent, $\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{o}}$ and $\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{e}}$ $(\mathrm{mg~L^{\-l}})$ are the concentrations of uranium at the initial and equilibrium states respectively, $\mathrm{v}$ (L) is the volume of the solution, and m (g) is the weight of the adsorbent.
In each experiment, we added $0.01\mathrm{g}$ DSHM-DAMN into $20~\mathrm{\mL}$ of uranium solution except isotherm studies. For isotherm studies, we added 0.01g DSHM-DAMN to $50~\mathrm{mL}$ uranium solution. The two experiments were conducted under different variables.
Adsorption tests in simulated seawater: typically, simulated seawater was prepared by dissolving $0.017\ \mathrm{g}$ of uranyl nitrate $\mathrm{[UO_{2}(N O_{3})_{2}]}\rangle$ ), $25.6\ \mathrm{g}$ sodium chloride (NaCl) and $0.193\mathrm{~g~}$ of sodium bicarbonate $\mathrm{(NaHCO_{3})}$ in deionized water, then diluting the solution to different initial uranium concentrations of 3, 10, 30, 50, 100, 300, 500 μg $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{~L~}}^{-1}$ , respectively. During the experiment, $0.01\ \mathrm{g}$ of DSHM-DAMN was added to 20 mL of artificial seawater and the mixture shaken for 24 h using a constant-temperature shaking bath. After adsorption was complete, the sample was centrifuged. Then, the supernatant solution was analyzed by ICP-MS.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Characterization of Materials in Fig 1 shows the results of double-shelled MIL-101(DSHM) and its derivatives DSHM-DAMN. From the FT-IR spectrum in Fig 1 (1), a new stretching vibration of adsorption appears at $2235~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which confirms the presence of C≡N groups.31 The three peaks appearing in the range of $3100{-}3500~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , are mainly caused by the N-H stretching vibration from two $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups. From the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns in Fig 1 (2), we have successfully synthesized MIL-101(Cr). The related peaks of the two other materials suggest that MIL-101 remains as the major framework structure with no apparent loss of crystallinity during the etching and post-modification process. On further comparison with pristine MIL-101,from the structure of double-shelled MIL-101 we observed octahedral morphologies32 and the double core-shells structure of DSHM (Fig.2).
We conducted a $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption experiment to investigate the surface area of three materials at a temperature of - $-196^{\circ}\mathrm{CF}$ rom Fig 1 (3). the three isotherms of Nitrogen sorption-desorption belong to Type-I curves, providing evidence of typical mesoporous materials. On comparison with pristine MIL-101, the BET surface area decreases from $2455\ \mathrm{~m}^{2}\ \mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ of MIL-101 to 2173 m ， of double-shelled MIL-101(DSHM), and $1620\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of DSHM-DAMN. The decrease in surface area of double-shelled MIL-101 accords with the fact that described previous. As the surface area decreases, more unsaturated metal sites are exposed. Also, the surface area decrease of DSHM-DAMN after post-modification shows the successfully grafting of organic groups.
