It is well known that the adsorption of uranium depends on pH is due to the different ionic species.33 We investigated the effect of pH on the adsorption of uranium in a pH range of 2 to 10. From Fig. 4, we note that uranium adsorption onto DSHM-DAMN is strongly pH-dependent, increasing sharply as the pH rises from 2 to 8; at $\mathrm{pH}\ 8$ , it reaches a maximum and decreases slightly at pH 9. When the pH is below 3, the adsorption of uranium is very low. At high pH, adsorption is most likely affected by the protonation and deprotonation of DAMN groups induced by the change of $\mathfrak{p H}$ . In addition, as the $\mathfrak{p H}$ increases the different forms of uranium from $\mathrm{UO}_{2}$ to $\left(\mathrm{UO}_{2}\right)_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{(UO_{2})_{3}(O H)}^{5+}$ , $\mathrm{(UO_{2})_{4}(O H)}^{7+}$ , and $\left(\mathrm{UO}_{2}\right)$ (OH)7- may favor the MOF adsorbents.34 The high adsorption performance of DSHM-DAMN possesses at $\mathsf{p H}8$ , enable its use in seawater; we conducted subsequent experiments at $\mathsf{p H}8$ .
