To investigate the effect of contact time on uranium adsorption, we conducted the adsorption experiments from $15~\mathrm{min}$ to $5\mathrm{h}$ at an initial uranium concentration of 100 mg ${\mathrm{~L}}^{-1}$ . As shown in Fig 5 (1), the adsorption rate of DSHM-DAMN is rapid in the initial $75\mathrm{min}$ , then slows down and finally reaches equilibrium at around 150 min. To further study the adsorption kinetics, we applied pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order models to fit the experiment data using the followed equations, as shown in Fig. 5(2) (3). Table 1 lists the correlation coefficients and model parameters, from which we note that the correlation coefficient value $\left(\operatorname{R}_{2}\right)$ is more than 0.99, which fits best with the pseudo-second-order; this shows that the adsorption mode of our material is chemisorption. 39 The DAMN groups that graft onto the surface behave like a chelating agent. From subsequent experiment, the mixture was stirred for $150\mathrm{min}$ to achieve complete adsorption of uranium.
To evaluate the uranium adsorption ability of DSHM-DAMN, we measured the adsorption isotherm. We conducted the experiment at three different temperatures (25, 35, $45~\%$ and added $0.01\mathrm{g}$ of the adsorbents to each of the 50 mL uranium solutions. The amount of uranium adsorbed by our adsorbent was measured under different initial uranyl concentrations 25 mg mL -1 to $400~\mathrm{{mg}~\mathrm{{mL}^{-1}}}$ at pH of 8.0. As shown in Fig. 6(1), we find that, with the increase of the initial uranium concentration, the uranium adsorption capacity steadily increases before reaching an equilibrium. At equilibrium, the saturated sorption capacity is as high as $601\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}$ under $25~\mathrm{^\circ}_{!}$ to our best knowledge, this is higher than all known MIL-101 based adsorbents, as shown in Table 3. To further investigate the adsorption mechanism behind the adsorption process, we describe the adsorption isotherms by using Langmiur and Freundlich models. In general, favoring the Langmiur model implies that the adsorption onto the adsorbents is by monolayer adsorption, in contrast to multilayer adsorption of the Freundlich model.40 Fig. 6(2) (3) displays the two adsorption isotherms and Table 2 lists the parameters. From these we see that the correlation coefficients $\left(\operatorname{R}_{2}\right)$ of Langmiur model are more than 0.98, that is, more than the $\mathbf{R}_{2}$ of Freundlich model, which means that the adsorption manner of DSHM-DAMN is closer to monolayer adsorption, which also corresponds to kinetic data.
Selectivity is an important factor for practical application. High removal of uranium depends on high selectivity of the adsorbent. To evaluate the selectivity of DSHM-DAMN towards uranium, we studied the uranium removal efficiency from an aqueous solution which contained eight coexisting ions that included $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ,K +, Mg2+, Na +, Ni2+ and As shown in Fig. 7, Compare to other ions, DSHM-DAMN exhibits significantly higher selectivity toward uranium in comparison to other ions. The removal rate of uranium reaches $85\%$ in the presence of other ions. And the removal rate of competitive ions is generally at a relatively low level, among them, only the removal rate of potassium ions reaches about $10\%$ , which is higher than that of other competing ions, but it is still far below the removal rate of uranium ions. We attribute the high selectivity of uranium to the groups that graft onto the DSHM to act as a hard base and the uranium act as hard acid. According to the HSAB principle that hard acids preferentially combined with hard alkalis, $^{41}\mathrm{UO}_{2}^{2+}$ first combining with DSHM-DAMN. Overall, the DSHM-DAMN shows high affinity for uranium and has promising potential as an adsorbent to recover uranium from seawater.
