We used XPS spectra to study the interaction environment and adsorption mechanism of DSHM-DAMN before and after uranium adsorption. As seen in Fig. 8 (1), with the XPS survey of DSHM-DAMN before adsorption, a new and strong high peak of U 4f appears in DSHM-DAMN-U. From Fig. 8 (2), we see the two high resolution peaks of U 4f with the binding energies of $393\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $382~\mathrm{eV}$ .42 These results confirm the existence of uranium onto DSHM-DAMN. As shown in Fig. 8 (3), we see two types of N in the samples at 399.6 and $398.4~\mathrm{eV}$ which are ascribed to C-N and C≡N groups.43 And also according to Fig. 8 (4), the binding energies of C-N and C≡N are at 400.5 eV, 399.5 eV, they shift in a positive direction of $0.9\mathrm{eV}$ and 1.1 eV, respectively. The positive shift of the two $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ binding energies means that the N atoms of both C-N and C≡N groups form part of the cheating reaction with uranium ions.44 So, according to the results of XPS spectra, the possible adsorption mechanism of uranium onto DSHM-DAMN is shown in Fig. 9.
