The uranium concentration in real seawater is only $3.3\upmu\up g\mathrm{~L~}^{-1}$ . It is important to ascertain the uranium adsorption efficiency of DSHM-DAMN under trace level uranium concentration, so we carried out a simulated seawater adsorption test. As seen from Fig. 10, the adsorption rate of uranium in simulated seawater is very high. The lowest removal rate under a concentration of only $3\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ still exceeds $80\%$ , and the highest removal rate is $98.7\%$ for a uranium concentration of $100~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . So, we believe that DSHM-DAMN exhibits excellent adsorption performance in seawater which promises a bright outlook for future application.
To investigate the reusability which is an important factor in practical application of DSHM-DAMN, five adsorption-desorption cycles were performed. During the cycling process, we used 0.1 M hydrochloric acid as the desorbent. As shown in Fig. 11, the adsorption performance of our materials drops very little after five cycles, which means our material is still stable after five regeneration cycles, indicating that DSHM-DAMN offers a good prospect of extraction uranium from seawater.
