## Conclusion
In this article, double-shelled hollow MIL-101 MOF materials were successfully fabricated by a step-by-step synthesis and subsequent acid etching. Modification of diaminomaleonitrile onto the hollow MOF material was achieved by using a facile
CUS method which successfully combines the characteristics of the hollow material with a large number of exposed and accessible unsaturated metal sites. By studying uranium adsorption kinetics, we find that the kinetic data fitted the pseudo-second-order model, indicating that adsorption occurs by chemisorption. The fitting of the Langmuir isotherm model indicates that monolayer adsorption occurs in the adsorption process. The maximum uranium adsorption capacity at room temperature is $601\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}$ under the optimum pH, exceeding all known amino groups grafted onto MIL-101 uranium adsorption materials. The adsorption capacity is high in comparison with other types of adsorbent materials (Table 4), which suggests that hollow nature of the material plays a key role. The high selectivity of uranium over competing ions and high adsorption rate at simulated seawater offer a promising prospect to introduce DSHM-DAMN in the extraction of uranium from seawater. Our report is the first to research hollow MOF materials for uranium adsorption research, and for exploring the application of hollow MOF materials in practice.
